window.docContents[166].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Introduction-to-Schur___s-lemma/#Chapter2___Discussion_Schur_intro":{"contents":"We will now prove our first result — Schur's lemma. Although it is very easy to prove, it is fundamental in the whole subject of representation theory.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Introduction to Schur's lemma","header":"Introduction to Schur's lemma","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Introduction-to-Schur___s-lemma/#Chapter2___Discussion_Schur_intro"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-representations-of-commutative-algebras-are-1-dimensional/#Chapter2___Corollary2___3___12___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Irreducible representations of commutative algebras are 1-dimensional\u0009Irreducible representations of commutative algebras are 1-dimensional","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-representations-of-commutative-algebras-are-1-dimensional/#Chapter2___Corollary2___3___12___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices--Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Every simple module over the auxiliary matrix subalgebra is linearly equivalent to a finite-index auxiliary type at some index.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary type depending on a field and a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary family of types indexed by the elements of a finite type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nScalar multiplication by an element of the auxiliary matrix subalgebra is multiplication by its corresponding diagonal entry.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nFor a positive natural-number parameter, the corresponding field-indexed auxiliary type has dimension one over the ground field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nEvery finite-index auxiliary type has dimension one over the ground field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nFor a positive exponent, the field-and-natural-number auxiliary type is simple over the corresponding adjoin-root algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nEach finite-index auxiliary type is simple over the auxiliary matrix subalgebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nFor a positive exponent, every simple module over the corresponding adjoin-root algebra is linearly equivalent to the field-and-natural-number auxiliary type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nFinite-index auxiliary types at distinct indices are not linearly equivalent over the auxiliary matrix subalgebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe adjoined root acts as zero on the field-and-natural-number auxiliary type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Example3.5.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Radicals of k[x]/(x^n) and upper triangular matrices\u0009Radicals of k[x]/(x^n) and upper triangular matrices\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices--Radicals-of-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_____LPAR_x___n_RPAR_-and-upper-triangular-matrices--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#chapter-03___section-3-8":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory","header":"3.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#chapter-03___section-3-8"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___27___-Representations-of-semidirect-products/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___27___-Representations-of-semidirect-products--Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A--Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under the displayed equality, the two auxiliary representations are isomorphic.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_semidirect_products; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.27.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary result whose formal statement is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_semidirect_products; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.27.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.27. Representations of semidirect products\u0009Classification of irreducible representations of semidirect products G ⋉ A\u0009Classification of irreducible representations of semidirect products G ⋉ A\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___27___-Representations-of-semidirect-products/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___27___-Representations-of-semidirect-products--Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A--Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-semidirect-products-G-___-A--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___8___heading-1":{"contents":"Given a representation V of a group G and a subgroup H \\subset G, there is a natural way to construct a representation of H. The restriction of V to H, \\operatorname{Res}_H^G V is the representation given by the vector space V, and the action \\rho_{\\operatorname{Res}_H^G V} = \\rho_V|_H.\n\nThere is also a natural, but less trivial, way to construct a representation of a group G given a representation V of its subgroup H.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Section 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction\u0009Section 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction","header":"5.8. Induced representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___8___heading-1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______--Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The endofunctor on quiver representations obtained from the auxiliary construction at a distinguished vertex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Definition6.6.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Definition6.6.3_maps; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.2.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Definition 6.6.3: Reflection functor F_i^+\u0009Definition 6.6.3: Reflection functor F_i^+\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______--Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Tor-and-Ext-in-abelian-categories-with-enough-projectives/#Chapter8___Discussion_after_Problem8___2___8":{"contents":"In a similar way one can define Tor and Ext groups in any abelian category which has enough projectives; i.e., any object is a quotient of a projective object (this condition insures that every object has a projective resolution).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Tor and Ext in abelian categories with enough projectives","header":"Tor and Ext in abelian categories with enough projectives","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Tor-and-Ext-in-abelian-categories-with-enough-projectives/#Chapter8___Discussion_after_Problem8___2___8"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/#Chapter8___Introduction":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u0009Chapter 8: Homological algebra","header":"Chapter 8: Homological algebra","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/#Chapter8___Introduction"}});